WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.081
Today, at the university.

00:00:04.181 --> 00:00:10.868
Every week, the faculties send the registrar's office the same questions: how many students, in which courses.

00:00:12.568 --> 00:00:16.570
So Mei Tanaka set up an agent that answers them at night, on its own.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:24.075
She set it up in an afternoon, with her personal key: a code that lets software sign in as her.

00:00:26.175 --> 00:00:30.985
Each month, Sam Okafor, who runs the data platform, checks the platform's log:

00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:34.625
who read the learner table, with every learner's name and email.

00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:39.229
One Sunday night, Mei read it 2,300 times.

00:00:41.529 --> 00:00:44.022
She was asleep. The reads were her agent's.

00:00:45.522 --> 00:00:50.064
Acting as her, it could read everything she can, though all it needed was counts.

00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:55.767
A cleverly worded question could have tricked it into handing names over.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:04.013
It called its model with another key of hers, paid from no one's budget, and no one saw what the calls carried.

00:01:05.713 --> 00:01:08.527
And if names ever leaked, the log would point at Mei.

00:01:10.027 --> 00:01:15.006
She did nothing wrong: a personal key can't hand over less than everything she has.

00:01:16.906 --> 00:01:18.294
Something had to change.

00:01:20.194 --> 00:01:24.575
So Sam gives the agent a name of its own, under the same rules as people.

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:29.533
Unity Catalog, where the platform keeps its rules,

00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:33.735
gives it only the grants its job needs, and stars where the names are.

00:01:35.435 --> 00:01:41.670
Unity Gateway, its gate to models, lets it call only the ones it's allowed, and counts what it spends.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:47.187
It worked. The faculties get the same answers every morning.

00:01:48.487 --> 00:01:56.003
Since then, it hasn't seen a single name, and its calls cost about 300 dollars a month, from the registrar's own budget.

00:01:57.703 --> 00:01:59.665
And the log says whose reads they were.

00:02:01.365 --> 00:02:07.328
Jun's census agent, which checks the count the university sends the government, works the same way:

00:02:07.528 --> 00:02:09.830
one set of rules for people and agents.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:24.325
The project's page for agents already says: never copy names, emails or student IDs into a prompt.

00:02:26.025 --> 00:02:28.607
But a rule an agent is told is a request.

00:02:29.707 --> 00:02:35.381
It can misread it, lose it in a long task, or be talked out of it by words hidden in what it reads.

00:02:37.481 --> 00:02:45.010
A rule the platform enforces holds, whatever the agent is told: it can't copy a name it only ever sees as stars.

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:53.657
So the team does both. It tells its agents the rules, and enforces them where the data and the models live.

00:02:55.357 --> 00:02:56.686
Two places keep them.

00:02:58.186 --> 00:03:00.905
Unity Catalog governs what agents touch:

00:03:01.305 --> 00:03:05.675
the tables, the functions they run as tools, and the log of who read what.

00:03:06.775 --> 00:03:11.806
Unity Gateway, part of the catalog, governs the models and tool servers they call.

00:03:15.906 --> 00:03:16.846
First, a name.

00:03:18.146 --> 00:03:22.773
An agent that helps one person, while they're there, can use that person's sign-in.

00:03:24.473 --> 00:03:28.584
One that works on its own, like Mei's at night, needs a name of its own.

00:03:30.084 --> 00:03:35.656
On Databricks, that's a service principal: an identity made for software, not for a person.

00:03:37.156 --> 00:03:43.077
Sam makes one for Mei's agent, the registrar agent, and grants it only what its job needs:

00:03:43.577 --> 00:03:47.521
it may read the core tables its answers come from, and write nowhere.

00:03:49.221 --> 00:03:56.782
Jun's census agent already has its own: it reads the core and the marts, and writes only to its own development schema.

00:03:58.482 --> 00:04:03.619
To stop an agent, Sam switches off its service principal, and everyone else works on.

00:04:05.719 --> 00:04:08.696
And Mei stays its owner: she answers for what it does.

00:04:12.396 --> 00:04:20.005
Then the masks. One policy shows stars in every column tagged direct, to everyone outside the registrar's office.

00:04:21.505 --> 00:04:27.092
It's written for all the account's users, and on Databricks, they include every service principal.

00:04:28.592 --> 00:04:33.123
The registrar agent isn't in the office's group: all it needs is counts.

00:04:34.223 --> 00:04:36.649
So it reads the learner table as Clara does:

00:04:36.849 --> 00:04:40.377
each learner's status, and stars where the names and emails are.

00:04:42.277 --> 00:04:46.752
Nobody wrote a rule for agents. The rule for people already covered them.

00:04:48.452 --> 00:04:52.316
A row filter works the same way, for an agent as for a person.

00:04:54.016 --> 00:04:56.520
And the learner code stays readable for counting,

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.627
so Jun's agent checks the census count, 18,412 students, without a single name.

00:05:07.327 --> 00:05:09.729
Agents' tools live in the catalog too.

00:05:10.429 --> 00:05:14.096
The census count the data team wrote once is a function there.

00:05:15.596 --> 00:05:18.677
Running it takes a grant, the same one a person needs.

00:05:19.377 --> 00:05:23.090
Jun's agent has it: it may run the count, and can't change it.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:29.148
And the tool servers agents call are registered there, through Unity Gateway,

00:05:29.348 --> 00:05:33.299
each with its own grant: an agent can reach these, and only these.

00:05:34.999 --> 00:05:39.308
Each gets the fewest rights: the registrar agent's tools only read.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:49.230
Then the models. In Unity Gateway, each model the university allows is a service in the catalog,

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.634
and only those granted it can call it.

00:05:53.334 --> 00:05:57.444
The registrar agent is granted one, and Mei's keys are taken back.

00:05:59.344 --> 00:06:02.700
Checks on each service look at what goes in and what comes out.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.647
One finds an email address in a prompt, and hides it or stops the call.

00:06:10.547 --> 00:06:13.214
Another looks for words written to trick the agent.

00:06:15.114 --> 00:06:17.641
A limit sets how many calls a minute it may make.

00:06:19.341 --> 00:06:26.257
And every call is counted under its name, so the registrar's office sees what its agent costs, in its own budget.

00:06:30.557 --> 00:06:32.779
A month later, Sam checks the log again.

00:06:33.479 --> 00:06:40.870
The same question, who read the learner table this month, lists Mei on weekday mornings, and the registrar agent at night.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.156
Whose reads were those? Now the log says.

00:06:46.856 --> 00:06:49.284
Lineage shows the tables its queries read.

00:06:50.784 --> 00:06:55.068
The gateway's own table lists every call it made, and the tokens it used.

00:06:56.568 --> 00:07:01.624
If something looks wrong, Sam can stop the agent alone, and nobody else stops with it.

00:07:03.524 --> 00:07:06.606
And no read of the agent's is taken for Mei's again.

00:07:10.306 --> 00:07:15.497
Why keep the rules where the data and the models live, and not only in what agents are told?

00:07:16.997 --> 00:07:22.767
Because there, they hold for every agent, every tool and every person, whatever anyone types.

00:07:24.467 --> 00:07:30.929
And because they're the rules people already work under, there's one set to keep, one log to read, and one review.

00:07:32.629 --> 00:07:35.174
Jun's census agent works under them too.

00:07:35.874 --> 00:07:41.567
The number the university sends the government, 18,412 students,

00:07:41.767 --> 00:07:45.498
is checked by an agent the team can see, limit and account for.

00:07:47.398 --> 00:07:52.096
And every morning, the faculties' answers wait under the registrar agent's own name.

00:07:53.796 --> 00:07:58.940
One set of rules for people and agents, enforced where the data and the models live.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.050
Next: the team's context and memory, governed like any data.
